  "name": "C1 ESTERASE INHIBITOR, PROTEIN QUANTITATION",
  "code": "C1EST",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">C1 inactivator deficiency is one of the commonest inherited complement deficiencies leading to hereditary angioedema. This deficiency is of two types :</span><br></p><div><ul><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 12px\">· &nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">Type I ( reduced serum levels of functionally </span><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">active </span><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">C1 inactivator)</span></p></li><li><p><span style=\"font-size: 12px\">· &nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">Type II (normal or elevated levels of functionally </span><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">inactive </span><span style=\"font-size: 13px\">C1 inactivator)<br>Acquired C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency is rare and all reported cases have been secondary to Lymphomasor Myelomas.</span></p></li></ul></div><div></div>",
